config VIDEO_MTK_CAM
	tristate "MediaTek MT6589 CAM/ISP control plane and IMGO DMA"
	depends on HAS_IOMEM && OF
	depends on ARCH_MEDIATEK || COMPILE_TEST
	select VIDEO_V4L2_SUBDEV_API
	select V4L2_ASYNC
	# No camera subdev can join a media graph without it: it owns the
	# media_device, the v4l2_device, the async notifier and the link
	# creation for the whole camera path.
	select VIDEO_MTK_CAM_GRAPH
	select MEDIA_CONTROLLER
	help
	  Say Y here if you want to use the MediaTek MT6589 CAM/ISP
	  control plane and IMGO output DMA.

	  This driver maps the CAM control registers at 0x15004000,
	  implements the documented software-reset handshake, block enable
	  and start/stop strobes, and programs the IMGO output DMA engine
	  from a videobuf2 capture queue.  It registers the CAM/ISP as a
	  subdev with both a sink and a source pad, and exposes the IMGO
	  output as a V4L2 capture video node with one format
	  (V4L2_PIX_FMT_UYVY, the YUV422 1-plane layout the CAM output
	  format field selects).

	  The image pipeline itself - the CPIPE stages, the memory sequencer
	  and the interrupt controllers - is NOT written, and no CPIPE stage
	  enable is programmed, because turning a stage on without writing
	  the pipeline would run it against unconfigured registers.  So the
	  video node registers cleanly, negotiates, allocates buffers and
	  starts, but the DMA engine is programmed rather than fed: there is
	  no CPIPE output for it to transfer.

	  On a board with no camera sensor - which is the case for the
	  Lenovo YOGA Tablet 8/10 this was written for, which has no sensor
	  node and no MIPI CSI-2 D-PHY in the device tree - STREAMON
	  succeeds and no frame ever completes, so DQBUF blocks until the
	  file is closed.  That is the intended inert behaviour, not a
	  defect.  See the file header.

	  To compile this driver as a module, choose M here: the module will
	  be called mtk-cam.
